1
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:07,130
Every test program release, every new product,

2
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:10,442
every stage consolidation comes down to one question:

3
00:00:10,462 --> 00:00:12,088
did anything change that shouldn't have?

4
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:17,123
Answering it usually means hours of manual comparison across limits,

5
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:21,105
bins, and yield. Slow, tedious, and prone to error.

6
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:27,895
Semiconductor STDF Sanity Checker automates the entire OLD versus NEW

7
00:00:27,908 --> 00:00:31,145
verification workflow, in one desktop application.

8
00:00:31,994 --> 00:00:36,324
Step one: load the two datasets. Native binary STDF,

9
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:40,424
Spotfire exports, and compressed cache files are all supported,

10
00:00:40,444 --> 00:00:43,144
and a cached dataset reloads in under a second.

11
00:00:44,003 --> 00:00:47,690
Step two: nine analysis features, each one click away.

12
00:00:49,716 --> 00:00:53,646
Feature one compares every test item between the two programs.

13
00:00:53,666 --> 00:00:55,979
In this demo, eight parameters are checked.

14
00:00:56,838 --> 00:00:59,730
Each test is classified: number change,

15
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:02,618
name change, limit change, unit change,

16
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:07,113
added, or removed. Here, Bias changed its unit from microamps to

17
00:01:07,126 --> 00:01:10,126
nanoamps, and its limits were rescaled to match.

18
00:01:10,982 --> 00:01:13,883
The full result is exported as a CSV report.

19
00:01:15,904 --> 00:01:18,416
Feature two compares bin states, die by die.

20
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:21,901
One hundred ninety-seven dies are aligned.

21
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,933
Six switched state, and yield moved from ninety-two point four to

22
00:01:25,946 --> 00:01:27,458
eighty-nine point three percent.

23
00:01:28,302 --> 00:01:31,557
OLD and NEW wafer maps sit beside a switch map,

24
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:34,582
which marks every die that moved from pass to fail,

25
00:01:34,602 --> 00:01:35,152
or back.

26
00:01:36,014 --> 00:01:40,114
Below, the Pareto charts and the yield comparison show exactly which

27
00:01:40,127 --> 00:01:40,927
bins changed.

28
00:01:42,902 --> 00:01:47,489
Delta Sigma Analysis grades the statistical shift of every common test.

29
00:01:48,346 --> 00:01:51,676
Each row shows delta sigma, sigma ratio,

30
00:01:51,696 --> 00:01:54,788
CPK, and the change in distribution shape,

31
00:01:54,808 --> 00:01:57,921
with an automatic verdict from no violation to full shift.

32
00:01:58,857 --> 00:02:02,899
For Rst_V, the mean rises by ten millivolts,

33
00:02:02,919 --> 00:02:05,719
and the distribution turns from normal to right-skewed.

34
00:02:06,578 --> 00:02:09,933
The overlaid CDF shows the shift against the limits,

35
00:02:09,953 --> 00:02:10,703
at a glance.

36
00:02:12,686 --> 00:02:16,278
Aligned analysis matches every die by its ECID,

37
00:02:16,299 --> 00:02:18,211
for a true die-to-die comparison.

38
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:24,508
In the S-plot, each point is one die: OLD value on the X axis,

39
00:02:24,528 --> 00:02:28,604
NEW value on the Y axis, with Pearson correlation computed for every

40
00:02:28,616 --> 00:02:29,079
test.

41
00:02:31,067 --> 00:02:35,134
The repeatability analyzer takes dies that were tested many times:

42
00:02:35,154 --> 00:02:37,079
fifty runs each, in this demo.

43
00:02:38,013 --> 00:02:40,993
For every test, it reports mean, sigma,

44
00:02:41,013 --> 00:02:43,713
equipment variation, CV, and Cp,

45
00:02:44,558 --> 00:02:47,338
and it classifies each time series as stable,

46
00:02:47,358 --> 00:02:49,596
drift, step shift, or cyclic.

47
00:02:51,628 --> 00:02:55,608
Feature six evaluates proposed limits before they reach production:

48
00:02:55,628 --> 00:03:00,303
load a limit file, and every die is re-binned and yield is recalculated.

49
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:04,830
The test summary lists limits, mean, CPK,

50
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:06,700
and fail rate for every parameter,

51
00:03:07,567 --> 00:03:10,809
and four tabs explain each one. Tab one:

52
00:03:10,829 --> 00:03:13,342
CDF, wafer map, and run chart.

53
00:03:14,211 --> 00:03:17,211
Tab two overlays several parameters on one chart.

54
00:03:18,071 --> 00:03:21,488
Tab three runs a site-to-site ANOVA. Here,

55
00:03:21,508 --> 00:03:23,383
it confirms there is no site bias.

56
00:03:24,246 --> 00:03:28,246
And tab four checks radial zones, from the wafer center to the edge.

57
00:03:30,223 --> 00:03:33,128
The cross-table engine pivots any two dimensions,

58
00:03:33,148 --> 00:03:36,872
such as lot, wafer, site, tester, or bin.

59
00:03:37,743 --> 00:03:42,443
Here, OLD hard bin against NEW: one hundred seventy-six dies stayed in

60
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:45,493
pass, while six moved from pass to open-short.

61
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:49,704
The same matrix is a spreadsheet you can copy straight into Excel.

62
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:57,595
For automotive programs, the AEC-Q001 PAT screener works on passing dies

63
00:03:57,608 --> 00:03:57,945
only.

64
00:03:58,794 --> 00:04:03,236
Robust limits are computed from the median and IQR of each test.

65
00:04:03,257 --> 00:04:06,919
For Rst_V, nineteen passing dies fall outside them.

66
00:04:07,766 --> 00:04:12,366
The linked CDF, wafer map, and run chart show where those mavericks sit.

67
00:04:14,343 --> 00:04:18,918
The drift analyzer compares pre- and post-stress data for every device.

68
00:04:19,774 --> 00:04:22,792
Tests with drift failures are listed with their shift,

69
00:04:22,812 --> 00:04:24,649
repeatability, and fail rate,

70
00:04:25,506 --> 00:04:29,286
and the plots separate genuine device drift from tester offset,

71
00:04:29,306 --> 00:04:31,481
using reference units as the baseline.

72
00:04:33,475 --> 00:04:38,080
Beyond the core platform, three advanced toolkits extend the analysis.

73
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:41,212
First, the Wafer Spatial Pattern AI analyzer.

74
00:04:42,074 --> 00:04:46,079
It folds every die into its position within the reticle shot.

75
00:04:46,099 --> 00:04:49,154
One position fails eighty-nine percent of the time,

76
00:04:49,174 --> 00:04:50,999
against a twenty-one percent baseline.

77
00:04:51,862 --> 00:04:55,587
It is flagged as a critical repeater, with a Z-score of fourteen point

78
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:58,062
six: a mask or lens defect.

79
00:04:58,939 --> 00:05:01,082
The pattern classifier scores edge-ring,

80
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:03,907
scratch, cluster, and other signatures,

81
00:05:03,927 --> 00:05:05,402
and recommends a root cause.

82
00:05:07,374 --> 00:05:12,224
The Automotive MPAT and GDBN screener is built for zero DPPM.

83
00:05:13,095 --> 00:05:17,700
Multivariate PAT catches dies that pass every individual limit,

84
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,920
yet fall outside the correlation ellipse between parameters.

85
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:25,941
Good-die-in-bad-neighborhood analysis flags passing dies surrounded by

86
00:05:25,954 --> 00:05:28,929
failures, and every die receives a risk tier.

87
00:05:30,898 --> 00:05:35,216
The Dual-Group Drift Cross-Analyzer covers reliability qualification

88
00:05:35,236 --> 00:05:38,323
across two test stations and up to eight readouts.

89
00:05:39,174 --> 00:05:42,024
Each failing die is traced through every readout,

90
00:05:42,866 --> 00:05:46,929
and a decision tree classifies it as true degradation or a suspected

91
00:05:46,941 --> 00:05:50,341
test issue, with the mathematical proof shown for each verdict.

92
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:56,773
Every result is one click away from a CSV report or a sixteen-by-nine

93
00:05:56,785 --> 00:05:57,710
PowerPoint deck.

94
00:05:58,583 --> 00:06:02,258
The spatial and automotive toolkits also run headless from the command

95
00:06:02,270 --> 00:06:06,383
line, exporting JSON and Excel for automated quality gates.

96
00:06:08,363 --> 00:06:12,218
The result: faster NPI, safer program releases,

97
00:06:12,238 --> 00:06:14,325
confident stage-elimination decisions,

98
00:06:15,186 --> 00:06:17,199
and zero manual transcription errors.

99
00:06:19,195 --> 00:06:24,000
Semiconductor STDF Sanity Checker. From hours of manual comparison,

100
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:24,820
to one click.

